                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0e473b76-2fae-3ccb-a62e-0ce4a9ed1624",
                        "entry_id": "f8730abc-42c0-3dee-ae45-78f3020e3e70",
                        "glosses": [
                            "het banaal-zijn"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d74a38d6-6fdb-444a-83e1-279f719be614",
                                "sense_id": "0e473b76-2fae-3ccb-a62e-0ce4a9ed1624",
                                "text": "'Eichmann in Jeruzalem - De banaliteit van het kwaad' is een boek van Hannah Arendt"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b92b53ab-8b65-44d1-982d-152622d65c93",
                        "source_entry_id": "f8730abc-42c0-3dee-ae45-78f3020e3e70",
                        "target_lang_word": "banality",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "banality",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "104a5c53-0a68-32f8-b692-f245ac064708",
                                        "entry_id": "6f309ed3-ed3a-3379-bd06-c387f35b59e8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The quality of being banal."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "76fca270-c682-4ee0-9b78-34c0a681548a",
                                                "sense_id": "104a5c53-0a68-32f8-b692-f245ac064708",
                                                "text": "The concept of the banality of evil came into prominence following the publication of Hannah Arendt's 1963 book Eichmann in Jerusalem: A Report on the Banality of Evil, which was based on the trial of Adolph Eichmann in Jerusalem."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "200ae23b-e4b6-316d-b0ea-6ddcda6b712d",
                                        "entry_id": "6f309ed3-ed3a-3379-bd06-c387f35b59e8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Something which is banal."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1eb184f6-f06c-34ba-bd3c-98a1afb3c50b",
                                        "entry_id": "6f309ed3-ed3a-3379-bd06-c387f35b59e8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A feudal right or obligation, especially the obligation for a peasant to grind grain at the lord's mill, or the profits accruing from such rights."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "historical",
                                            "rare",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a50a350f-656f-40d9-9cd4-1f4478d69b90",
                                                "sense_id": "1eb184f6-f06c-34ba-bd3c-98a1afb3c50b",
                                                "text": "The law of banality, one of the most oppressive products of feudalism, was revived for the advantage of the nobility."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b563f39b-c914-4ef8-b9ef-17ec43b56de9",
                                                "sense_id": "1eb184f6-f06c-34ba-bd3c-98a1afb3c50b",
                                                "text": "Other banalities included the lord's exclusive right to hunt over the land, his monopoly over fishing, and his right to keep the dove-cote whose feathery occupants ate a peasant's standing crops."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dfc31caa-5d95-4678-acd4-8f90b7fe0821",
                                                "sense_id": "1eb184f6-f06c-34ba-bd3c-98a1afb3c50b",
                                                "text": "In fact corvées, champarts, and rights of banality not only continued but had been increased in the course of the seventeenth century."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3ac87c27-30b4-47c2-a8b6-f80e38e7ca6e",
                        "source_entry_id": "f8730abc-42c0-3dee-ae45-78f3020e3e70",
                        "target_lang_word": "platitude",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "platitude",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e3b89845-0113-34f9-9d02-d47dbd01902a",
                                        "entry_id": "5915aebe-d917-3c6d-80e5-88e060b6cba4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An often-quoted saying that is supposed to be meaningful but has become unoriginal or hackneyed through overuse."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6c5257ac-6f10-48fa-886c-ad2f13a43b3a",
                                                "sense_id": "e3b89845-0113-34f9-9d02-d47dbd01902a",
                                                "text": "Beauty, I suppose, opens the heart, extends the consciousness. It is a platitude, of course."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2686d31b-6312-4722-b4b1-eb26c64e44eb",
                                                "sense_id": "e3b89845-0113-34f9-9d02-d47dbd01902a",
                                                "text": "Semiramis was the first woman to invent eunuchs and women have had sympathy for them ever since; […] and women can tell them what they can't tell other men. And Ivor, suddenly cheered by laughing at his absurd platitudes, and finding himself by the door, was going from the room."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "77f436cf-b336-4dc7-8a0e-f5a38f84bd12",
                                                "sense_id": "e3b89845-0113-34f9-9d02-d47dbd01902a",
                                                "text": "For most of the past three decades, the natural world was treated almost as an afterthought by world leaders. If discussed at all, it was with platitudes about the need to save polar bears and tigers."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4e3298d4-aac9-3e5f-9798-0dc7fc177b19",
                                        "entry_id": "5915aebe-d917-3c6d-80e5-88e060b6cba4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A claim that is trivially true, to the point of being uninteresting."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f9e668f7-70fd-4933-a117-d85cad1d4158",
                                                "sense_id": "4e3298d4-aac9-3e5f-9798-0dc7fc177b19",
                                                "text": "The synthesis which he helped to effect was so successful that this aspect of his work escaped notice in the last century: all that Britomart stands for was platitude to our fathers. It is platitude no longer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "de4b1630-d366-42f1-9067-59ba96cffd41",
                                                "sense_id": "4e3298d4-aac9-3e5f-9798-0dc7fc177b19",
                                                "text": "After explaining myself sufficiently, I now offer my own platitude: I believe that the institution of the cabaret has the right to exist only so long as it bears the character of dilettantism and improvisation."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "68d5a969-79f0-4aeb-9447-e5f9e2592232",
                                                "sense_id": "4e3298d4-aac9-3e5f-9798-0dc7fc177b19",
                                                "text": "Indeed, in the ownership scenario the idealization is supported in a much thinner manner: we start with a platitude that characterizes individuals as coowners (that they are de facto seen as either property holders themselves or otherwise as […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5eba65de-7f15-3dc6-a5bb-fe00da294902",
                                        "entry_id": "5915aebe-d917-3c6d-80e5-88e060b6cba4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Flatness; lack of change, activity, or deviation."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "51c7bbdc-1728-433f-9f56-6b72e52ca83f",
                                                "sense_id": "5eba65de-7f15-3dc6-a5bb-fe00da294902",
                                                "text": "The former figures the typical prairie landscape-poet who stops at the correction line (which itself literally denies the platitude of flatness) to notice the ever-present wind."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f1d2c26f-90e1-4a25-8127-78311f2d0603",
                                                "sense_id": "5eba65de-7f15-3dc6-a5bb-fe00da294902",
                                                "text": "With the photograph, we enter into flat death. One day, leaving one of my classes, someone said to me with disdain, \"You talk about death very flatly.\" — As if the horror of Death were not precisely its platitude!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "faaf02fa-d2c0-4052-be02-9719b428a15f",
                                                "sense_id": "5eba65de-7f15-3dc6-a5bb-fe00da294902",
                                                "text": "Though we do not have a traditional correspondence theory, which sets up a structural similarity relation between statements and facts, we have the truth of sentences determined by word-to-world relations, in particular, relations between linguistic items and objects. This reflects the platitude behind correspondence, but the Tarskian appartatus gives the platitude more substance."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "42789994-53d8-3e6b-94c5-6f05fab1005b",
                                        "entry_id": "5915aebe-d917-3c6d-80e5-88e060b6cba4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Unoriginality; triteness."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cee009a1-1e7c-45f4-9f5e-b48516559f12",
                                                "sense_id": "42789994-53d8-3e6b-94c5-6f05fab1005b",
                                                "text": "seemly platitude, flat-footed ordinariness, and well-enacted upper working class respectability cancel out any turpitude, exhilarating tension or satanic glamour a casino might be expected to have."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e8afc7b0-cf9d-4b5a-b498-bb77019ac0dc",
                                                "sense_id": "42789994-53d8-3e6b-94c5-6f05fab1005b",
                                                "text": "After a passage of what we feel to be true poetry, there follows, inevitably, a passage of platitude which no critical pre-judgment can force us to admire;"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c23c7564-988a-4e99-940c-bfe07381c76d",
                                                "sense_id": "42789994-53d8-3e6b-94c5-6f05fab1005b",
                                                "text": "Of the true steady-going German nature the bane is, as I remarked, flat commonness; there seems no end to its capacity for platitude; it has neither the quick perception of the Celt to save it from platitude, nor the strenuousness of the Norman;"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d9617e8-51ac-4812-96b8-5548d9bf0e64",
                                                "sense_id": "42789994-53d8-3e6b-94c5-6f05fab1005b",
                                                "text": "More damning, this 'flat-pack kit' causes us to decline into platitude and predictability, denies us from real political intellectual purchase and, as result, standard theoretical ideas become our ideological comforters."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a7a508d0-3b53-46a6-b160-7dc5385d9609",
                        "source_entry_id": "f8730abc-42c0-3dee-ae45-78f3020e3e70",
